WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.460
What relaxes an artery?

2
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:12.340
In 1980, Robert Furchgott and John Zawadzki mounted rings of rabbit aorta in an

3
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:18.000
organ bath. Some rings kept their endothelium, the single layer of cells that lines

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.900
the vessel. In others, it had been rubbed off.

5
00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:23.903
They added acetylcholine.

6
00:00:24.563 --> 00:00:28.183
It relaxed the intact rings but not the rubbed ones.

7
00:00:28.843 --> 00:00:31.963
So the lining was sending something to the muscle beneath it.

8
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:34.903
That something lasted only seconds.

9
00:00:35.983 --> 00:00:41.663
In 1986, Furchgott proposed that it is the gas nitric oxide, NO.

10
00:00:42.723 --> 00:00:44.883
Ignarro independently proposed the same.

11
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:51.697
Acetylcholine binds a GPCR on the endothelial cell, and calcium rises.

12
00:00:52.937 --> 00:00:57.157
Calcium, bound to calmodulin, switches on nitric oxide synthase.

13
00:00:57.737 --> 00:01:00.637
It makes NO from the amino acid arginine.

14
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:05.237
NO is small and uncharged, so it crosses membranes freely.

15
00:01:05.957 --> 00:01:09.037
It cannot be aimed, and it reacts away within seconds.

16
00:01:09.337 --> 00:01:11.337
So only near neighbors receive it.

17
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:19.030
In the muscle cell, NO binds the iron of a heme group in soluble guanylyl cyclase.

18
00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.710
It changes shape and makes cGMP from GTP.

19
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:32.010
The cGMP switches on protein kinase G. The muscle relaxes, and the vessel widens.

20
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:37.250
A phosphodiesterase, PDE5, ends the signal.

21
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:42.230
Outside the eye, cGMP carries its signal by rising.
